WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.941
Played by writer-director Kevin Smith and
his childhood best friend Jason Mewes, Jay

00:00:04.941 --> 00:00:10.219
and Silent Bob have been cult favorites for
over two decades, appearing in movies, TV

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:13.950
shows, cartoons, and comic books, with no
signs of stopping.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.470
But what's the real story behind their popularity?

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.260
Here's a look at the untold truth of Jay and
Silent Bob.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.040
Jay is Jay

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.830
Smith based the loud, obnoxious, and aggressive
character of Jay on none other than…Jason

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:27.850
Mewes himself.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.200
Mewes told The Skinny,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.870
"When we did Clerks, I was 18, 19.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.460
That's how I used to act, exactly.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.370
I didn't have any filter, I used to say stuff
exactly like that, [like] 'what up' this and

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.990
'what up' that, that's stuff I had said over
the years that Kevin had written down."

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.950
"Smoking weed, smoking weed.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.910
Doin' coke, drinking beers.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.870
Drinking beers, beers, beers.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.830
Rolling fatties, smoking blunts.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.239
Who smokes the blunts?

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:51.940
We smoke the blunts!"

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.220
Disaster movie

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.820
Smith famously self-financed Clerks, which
had a paltry $27,000 budget, by selling off

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.430
his extensive collection of comic books, pilfering
from his college fund, and then maxing out

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.430
a bunch of credit cards.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.030
And a natural disaster also helped.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:13.170
Smith explains in Stephen Lowenstein's 2002
interview collection, My First Movie, that

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:17.470
the rest of the budget came from an insurance
settlement for a destroyed car he and Mewes

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.470
shared.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.500
Mewes warned Smith that the area was going
to flood, and that they should move the car.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.049
Smith said he didn't want to.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.700
Good thing they didn't.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.310
Thank you for smoking

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.369
A standout scene in Clerks featured an anti-smoking
tirade delivered by a guy who turns out to

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.610
have a self-interest in the matter: a representative
of Chewlies, a fictional gum manufacturer.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:42.020
"It's that kind of mentality that allows the
cancer-producing industry to thrive.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.900
Of course we're all going to die someday!

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.390
But do we have to pay for it?"

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.150
Ironically, Smith himself was a non-smoker
until he filmed Clerks.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.409
Puffing on-screen as Silent Bob was enough
to get him hooked, and he eventually became

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:56.040
a two-pack a day smoker.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.829
Luckily, he figured out a way to quit.

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:01.100
"Lemme see, I've been quit smoking now, off
cigarettes for three years.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.579
And it was very easy, I quit smoking but I
just simply started smoking weed instead.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:07.579
Jay vs. Scott Evil

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:11.770
Smith made Clerks himself, so he was free
to cast anybody he wanted.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:16.599
But because Gramercy Pictures bankrolled the
quasi-sequel Mallrats, Smith had to deal with

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:18.540
some corporate interference.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.760
Studio executives were opposed to letting
Mewes play Jay again, and instead wanted the

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.510
more experienced and bankable Seth Green to
step into the role.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.720
Smith convinced studio executives that Mewes
was right for the role that he originated,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.780
but the studio still kept Green on call for
the duration of the shoot as an understudy

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.670
should things not work out with Mewes.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:42.090
To add insult to injury, the studio also refused
to pay for Mewes to travel to the set in Minnesota

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.690
or for his hotel room during the shoot.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.200
Studio executives even showed up to the first
day of filming, fully prepared to fire Mewes

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.050
if they didn't think he was good enough.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.940
Smith made Mewes aware of the situation, prompting
him to give as good a performance as he could—and

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.830
the suits finally approved.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.780
"Now, son of Jor-El, kneel before Zod!

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.380
Snootchie bootchies!

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.060
Haha!"

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.440
Dogmatic

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:12.890
After Gramercy's reluctance to cast Mewes,
he showed up to the set of Smith's next movie

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.970
extra prepared.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:19.060
He memorized all his lines before shooting
on Dogma began … along with everybody else's.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.960
He'd memorized the entire script.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.800
This was because Smith met with Mewes before
rehearsals and told him that he needed to

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.240
do his best work, because not only was Dogma
the biggest production they'd ever embarked

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.159
on, but they had "real actors in the movie"
this time, namely the legendary Alan Rickman.

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:39.050
Mewes showed up and gave his performance everything
he had because, as he put it, he didn't want

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.500
to "piss off that Rickman dude."

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.480
"Bow down, stupid!"

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:44.830
Life imitating art

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:49.489
Despite memorizing the entire script for Dogma,
Mewes still had trouble filming the movie

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:52.970
because he was deeply addicted to heroin at
the time of shooting.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.220
In a series of blog posts, Smith detailed
the times during the shoot when Mewes was

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:00.300
especially far gone, because it's visible
in the film.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.049
In the scene where the apostle Rufus is delivering
his backstory outside the Mooby's, for example,

00:04:05.049 --> 00:04:09.879
Jay struggles to keep from nodding off, a
telltale sign of a heroin high.

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:13.500
Smith worked tirelessly for years to try and
help his friend kick the habit.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.150
After several failed attempts, Smith invited
Mewes to live with him and his family.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:23.490
But Mewes returned the favor by stealing Smith's
bank card and using the money to buy heroin.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:27.069
Smith didn't give up, though, checking Mewes
into rehab time and again.

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:32.619
He has reportedly been drug-free since 2010
and is now happily married with a child.

00:04:32.619 --> 00:04:34.749
Clerks: The Animated Series

00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:40.169
In 2000, Disney-owned ABC commissioned an
animated series based on Clerks.

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:44.139
While the film's dialogue and plot points
are downright filthy, that kind of thing just

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:47.069
wasn't going to fly on primetime network television.

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:51.749
As a result, the family-friendly network required
some alterations from the source material.

00:04:51.749 --> 00:04:56.550
Among them: Jay and Silent Bob are not drug
dealers on Clerks: The Animated Series.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:00.930
Rather, they're just kind of pranksters who
hang out outside the Quick Stop, and occasionally

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.669
sell fireworks to kids.

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:03.669
"Hi, I'm Jay."

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:04.979
"And I'm Silent Bob."

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:06.370
"And I'm Charles Barkley."

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.060
"What the hell are you doing here?"

00:05:09.869 --> 00:05:13.639
Only two episodes aired before ABC canceled
it.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:14.800
Scream!

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.039
Over the years, Jay and Silent Bob have made
a number of guest appearances in television

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:25.149
and film, including on on Degrassi: The Next
Generation and in the 2000 film Scream 3.

00:05:25.149 --> 00:05:29.639
Miramax studio head Bob Weinstein called Smith
and told him he had an idea for a Jay and

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:31.800
Silent Bob cameo in the movie.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.440
Smith came up with some lines for the scene
on the spot, while Mewes was down because

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.960
he liked the $300 per diem and free T-shirt.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.229
He told Smith, "We should do this all the
time.

00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:45.389
We can pop up in every horror movie, and get
three hundred bucks a day and a shirt!"

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:46.580
Thanks for watching!

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.389
